        "content": "**What the product does:** Per the Show HN post, Opslane is an open-source agent that combines error tracking and session recording to identify user-facing bugs, investigate them, and only open a PR \"if it can verify the fix.\" Its SDK captures errors, console logs, network requests, and recordings; it detects \"rage clicks, dead clicks, and abandoned forms,\" ranks issues by user impact, self-hosts via Docker Compose, and ships an MCP server so devs can query it from Claude Code.\n\n**Representative comments:**\n\n1. **Privacy criticism (rkagerer):** Calls the recording aspect a dealbreaker — \"The end-user recording aspect makes this an absolute non-starter for me.\" He argues poor bug handling is usually \"a symptom of corporate culture or business process,\" not something AI can patch.\n\n2. **Support for the concept (satvikpendem):** Defends session recording by analogy to retail store cameras, noting tools like PostHog, Microsoft Clarity, and Hotjar already do it. Separately argues \"Most people won't file bugs, they'll just churn\" — so bug reports alone miss real issues.\n\n3. **Competitive context (staticshock):** Points out PostHog already has similar \"Scouts\" agents that review event data and session replays and open PRs, while encouraging the founder (who took it as validation, not a slight).\n\n4. **Probing question (jasonshen):** Asks what \"continuously learning about your product\" means in practice — answered by the founder, who explains recordings reveal which screens/actions matter most for prioritizing issues.\n\n**Notable founder replies:** Only web is supported today (mobile on the roadmap), sessions are recorded with rrweb, and the creator argues recording data is info you already have or could easily get."
