        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "## Junie Local — Overview\n\n**What it is:** Junie Local is JetBrains' first on-device version of its Junie AI coding agent, running entirely on a Mac with no cloud connection. The blog post is dated August 2026 (URL path `/junie/2026/08/junie-local-launch/`; no explicit date appears in the article body).\n\n**How it works:**\n- Run `/local` inside Junie — the model downloads, a local server starts, and the agent switches over with no manual configuration.\n- Model: Qwen3.6-27B at 4-bit quantization, ~20 GB download, tuned by JetBrains against its own agent loop (they picked 3.6 over newer 3.8 because 3.8 requires reasoning mode, making tasks ~4x slower).\n- Hardware requirement: an M5 Mac with 64 GB of RAM. The team admits this is steep but says lowering the memory floor is a priority.\n\n**Key claims:**\n- Free: \"no registration, no subscription, no credits, and no card required.\"\n- Privacy/offline: \"No tokens, no quota, no code ever leaving your machine,\" and it works with no network once weights are on disk.\n- Performance: on JetBrains' private test set, Qwen3.6-27B scored on par with Sonnet 4.5 (10,000-token reasoning limit); GPT-5 at medium effort scored slightly higher — with the local model shipping with reasoning disabled.\n- Existing features carry over unchanged: Plan mode, live prompting, guidelines, skills, and `/commands`.\n- Optimizations include M5 Neural Accelerator 8-bit instructions (~40% prefill throughput gain), KV-cache reuse, and speculative decoding (~2x generation speed).\n\n**Platform status:** Mac-only at launch; JetBrains has working prototypes for DGX Spark and RTX 5090 and is eyeing 24 GB GPUs.\n\n**Which IDEs:** The article doesn't list specific IDEs — it only references the Junie agent generally (a related linked post describes Junie as working \"inside the IDE and the terminal\")."
      }
    ]
  },
